From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: accept S-R uploads

While the S-R mode is active, a flow entering through a user port of the
rtl8_4s switch can be told apart from the frames of every other port and
VLAN: the switch puts its port's upload S-tag on them, and an identity
(an XLT rule on that S-tag and on the C-tag form of the port's class,
installed by the mode) gives those frames, and those alone, an L3
interface of their own. This patch accepts such uploads, keyed on that
L3 interface instead of the conduit's, which every port and VLAN of the
switch shares.

An upload from a switch port is classified to its port's untagged class
U(p) when the flowtable gives it no VLAN key, or to T(p, v) when it gives
an exact 802.1Q key v with its pop; a second tag, an 802.1ad key, an
inexact or zero VID and a PCP match are declined before, as for every
flow. It is judged in two steps:

- lockless (ppe_sr_offer_pre()): the offer's ticket gives the S-R mode, the
  port awaits no resync (else -EAGAIN), its conduit is the S-R one and
  says ACTIVE, the port is in the S-R port mask and forwards, and the
  topology published to offers gives it a class;
- under the lock (ppe_sr_offer_lock()), after the ticket: the port still
  awaits no resync, the switch still says ACTIVE (else the disarm of the
  statistics-time check is queued), the class comes out of the topology
  as it is now as it came out of the one judged on (else -EAGAIN), and
  the class has a live identity, whose L3 interface becomes the flow's
  source L3 interface.

Only the second accepts; both are pure (ppe_sr_eng.c) and tested in user
space with every gate, class, scope and race between them. Between them,
the flow must be SNAT towards an EDMA port that is not a switch conduit
(the WAN; a PPPoE upload included, which this makes possible), the
ingress and egress EDMA devices must differ, and WP3's scope must hold
the class and the client (the source address); back to a switch port,
DNAT and out of scope are declined. Every S-R decline is counted by
reason, every accepted upload by identity.

An accepted upload is on its identity's flow list from before its
hardware add until it is forgotten, on every path: a failed add unlinks
it, a flow left in hardware by one stays linked (and the degraded latch
is S-R's FAULT, which keeps the identity's L3 interface as it is), a
lost one is unlinked and is FAULT too. The S-R flushes no longer drop
every flow: an identity's flush drops its list and reports what stays on
it, a port flush the S-R flows of those ports, and a scope change the
S-R flows the new scope excludes (it no longer marks every port dirty:
it bumps the generation, so no offer judged under the old scope is
added, and the resync makes the identities follow).

Also: the arm publishes its topology before turning offers on, so that
an offer judged while ACTIVE always finds one; the dirty marks, the S-R
port mask and the conduit pointer are stored with WRITE_ONCE() for the
lockless judgement; the PPPoE and source MAC L3 interfaces are asserted
below the identities'; offload_flows shows "sr up id I l3_if N" for an
S-R upload; the idle verify checks every upload against its identity
(the identity in use with the flow's class and L3 interface, live, the
upload on its list) and disarms on a mismatch while ACTIVE; the status
gives each identity's uploads and the accepted offers by direction.
S-R downloads are still declined.

Signed-off-by: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
